<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    allowBackup="false" (AndroidManifest.xml) already blocks cloud backup entirely, but it does
    NOT cover device-to-device transfer (Android 12+ "Copy your data" / D2D restore), which is
    governed separately by the <device-transfer> block below. Room's shellwave.db is where every
    CredentialEntity row lives (see core/db/entities/CredentialEntity.kt) - the AES-GCM
    ciphertext plus the alias naming which Android Keystore key sealed it. The key itself never
    leaves the Keystore (hardware-backed, per-device) so the ciphertext alone is unusable on
    another device, but excluding the database matters anyway: hosts/scripts/etc. carry little
    value without their credentials.

    Room opens in WAL journal mode by default, so shellwave.db is never alone - confirmed on
    device (`run-as ... ls -la databases/`) that shellwave.db-shm and shellwave.db-wal sit beside
    it in the same databases/ directory from the moment the DB is first opened. Recent writes -
    including a newly-sealed CredentialEntity row not yet checkpointed back into the main file -
    live in -wal, not shellwave.db. A `path="shellwave.db"` exclude is an exact-file match, not a
    prefix, so it does NOT cover -wal/-shm: naming only the main file would leave exactly the
    freshest credential ciphertext eligible for transfer. This dialect has no glob/prefix
    matching, but domain="database" path="." matches the whole database directory recursively,
    which is both correct (catches -wal/-shm, and any future companion file the journal mode
    adds) and simple: this app has exactly one database, so excluding the domain is excluding
    the database, not a broader blast radius.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude
            domain="database"
            path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude
            domain="database"
            path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
